# Pure fragment symbols

Editor adapters supply their current document text, including unsaved buffers.
This module performs no I/O and imports no editor APIs. Coordinates are zero-based
raw UTF-16 columns; occurrences cover the entire tag, while edits cover its name.
The engine owns both lexical grammars. Markdown-it decides which fences and
inline regions are meaningful, using the same default parser as generation.


## Contracts and safe inputs

Collection retains duplicate declarations and orphan appends: an editor needs
these even when generation rejects them. Rename requires an unambiguous base.
Canonical paths make edits usable by adapters; portable case-insensitive duplicate
paths are rejected instead of producing overlapping edits. NUL source is rejected
before Markdown-it can replace it and hide the original spelling.

```ts : <<symbol contracts>>=
import MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
import Token = require('markdown-it/lib/token');
import StateInline = require('markdown-it/lib/rules_inline/state_inline');
import StateBlock = require('markdown-it/lib/rules_block/state_block');
import { SourceDocument, FragmentTag, parseFragmentHeader, safeRelativePath, scanFragmentTags } from './engine';

export type FragmentOccurrence = {
    name: string; path: string; line: number; column: number; endColumn: number;
    kind: 'definition' | 'append' | 'reference' | 'mention';
};
export type FragmentRenameEdit = {
    path: string; line: number; startColumn: number; endColumn: number; newText: string;
};
type InlineTag = FragmentTag;
```

Symbol identity is the occurrence's exact name, not a separate symbol object.
One document can contribute many occurrences of any of the four kinds. For a
changed rename, each occurrence of the old name yields one name-only edit;
unrelated occurrences remain part of the safety check.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class SourceDocument {
        string path
        string text
    }
    class FragmentOccurrence {
        string name
        string path
        number line
        number column
        number endColumn
        kind definition or append or reference or mention
    }
    class FragmentRenameEdit {
        string path
        number line
        number startColumn
        number endColumn
        string newText
    }
    SourceDocument "1" --> "0..*" FragmentOccurrence : collected from text
    FragmentOccurrence "1" --> "0..1" FragmentRenameEdit : matching old name
    FragmentRenameEdit "0..*" --> "1" SourceDocument : targets name range
```

Validation produces fresh objects and never changes caller-owned buffers.
Neither collection nor planning consults files on disk.

```ts : <<symbol document validation>>=
function normalizeDocuments(documents: SourceDocument[]): SourceDocument[] {
    const paths = new Set<string>();
    return documents.map(document => {
        const path = safeRelativePath(document.path);
        if (!path) { throw new Error(`Invalid source path: ${document.path}`); }
        const key = path.toLowerCase();
        if (paths.has(key)) { throw new Error(`Duplicate source document: ${path}`); }
        if (document.text.includes('\0')) { throw new Error(`NUL source text: ${path}`); }
        paths.add(key);
        return { path, text: document.text };
    });
}
```

Every range is built from a scanner offset, never by searching for a fragment
name. Repeated tags and names embedded in other names therefore remain distinct.

```ts : <<symbol occurrence construction>>=
function occurrence(tag: FragmentTag, path: string, line: number, offset: number,
    kind: FragmentOccurrence['kind']): FragmentOccurrence {
    return { name: tag.name, path, line, column: offset + tag.start,
        endColumn: offset + tag.end, kind };
}
```

## Named fences retain raw positions

Only headers accepted by the shared grammar contribute definitions or body
references. Filename and settings text are never scanned. The opening marker
anchors header positions even when a language or filename repeats the name.
Body mapping matches engine inspection: Markdown removes prefixes and may expand
part of a tab, but the suffix beginning at a tag remains unchanged.

Fence symbol collection is presented in [Fragments as editor symbols](fragments_as_editor_symbols.html).

## Prose mentions use inline parsing, not rendered text

A small inline rule records source offsets before Markdown decoding. Default
escape handling consumes escaped openers before this rule sees them. Markdown's
link parser skips destinations and titles, but parses labels normally. Bare
words, entities that render as tags, and reference-link definitions are not
mentions. Inline code is deliberately included, with escapes tested on its raw
contents, not its whitespace-normalized rendered value. Image alt text is
conservatively excluded: Markdown-it reparses it with a separate coordinate
origin. This prevents returning an edit into unrelated text before the image.

```ts : <<symbol inline scanner>>=
function mentionParser(tags: InlineTag[], source: string): MarkdownIt {
    const md = new MarkdownIt();
    md.inline.ruler.before('text', 'fragment_mentions', (state, silent) => {
        if (!state.src.startsWith('\<<', state.pos) || state.src[state.pos - 1] === '\\') { return false; }
        const tag = scanFragmentTags(state.src.slice(state.pos, state.posMax))[0];
        if (!tag || tag.start !== 0) { return false; }
        if (!silent) {
            if (state.src === source) { tags.push({ ...tag, start: state.pos, end: state.pos + tag.end }); }
            state.pending += state.src.slice(state.pos, state.pos + tag.end);
        }
        state.pos += tag.end;
        return true;
    });
    installCodeMentions(md, tags, source);
    return md;
}
```

Delegate backtick matching to Markdown-it itself so unmatched delimiters,
multiple-backtick spans and embedded backticks keep the parser's semantics.
Only successful code spans contribute tags; silent link lookahead contributes
nothing. The original raw slice retains both backslashes and UTF-16 offsets.

```ts : <<symbol inline code scanner>>=
function installCodeMentions(md: MarkdownIt, tags: InlineTag[], source: string): void {
    const backticks: (state: StateInline, silent: boolean) => boolean =
        require('markdown-it/lib/rules_inline/backticks');
    md.inline.ruler.at('backticks', (state, silent) => {
        const start = state.pos, count = state.tokens.length;
        const accepted = backticks(state, silent);
        if (accepted && !silent && state.src === source && state.tokens.length > count &&
            state.tokens[state.tokens.length - 1].type === 'code_inline') {
            for (const tag of scanFragmentTags(state.src.slice(start, state.pos))) {
                tags.push({ ...tag, start: start + tag.start, end: start + tag.end });
            }
        }
        return accepted;
    });
}
```

Inline token content strips heading markers and container prefixes. Align each
complete trimmed content line with its original line, not individual names.
This also handles closing heading markers and partially consumed leading tabs.
If a line cannot be aligned exactly, conservatively omit its mentions.

```ts : <<symbol prose mapping>>=
function proseOccurrences(token: Token, path: string, lines: string[]): FragmentOccurrence[] {
    if (!token.map) { return []; }
    const tags: InlineTag[] = [];
    const md = mentionParser(tags, token.content);
    md.inline.parse(token.content, md, {}, []);
    const contentLines = token.content.split('\n');
    return tags.flatMap(tag => {
        const before = token.content.slice(0, tag.start);
        const index = before.split('\n').length - 1;
        const line = token.map![0] + index, content = contentLines[index];
        const column = lines[line].indexOf(content.trim());
        if (column < 0) { return []; }
        const prefix = content.length - content.trimStart().length;
        const lineStart = before.lastIndexOf('\n') + 1;
        return [occurrence(tag, path, line, column - prefix - lineStart, 'mention')];
    });
}
```

Table inline tokens have no map. Mirror Markdown-it's cell splitting while
retaining a raw UTF-16 column for every surviving code unit. Its table rule
removes the backslash immediately before a pipe (even after another backslash).
Trim cells only after splitting, and keep repeated or empty cells positional.

```ts : <<symbol table cell splitting>>=
type TableCell = { content: string; columns: number[] };
type TableSource = TableCell & { line: number };
function tableCells(raw: string, offset: number): TableCell[] {
    const cells: TableCell[] = [];
    let cell: TableCell = { content: '', columns: [] };
    for (let index = 0; index < raw.length; index++) {
        const char = raw[index];
        if (char === '|' && raw[index - 1] !== '\\') {
            cells.push(cell);
            cell = { content: '', columns: [] };
            continue;
        }
        if (char === '|') {
            cell.content = cell.content.slice(0, -1);
            cell.columns.pop();
        }
        cell.content += char;
        cell.columns.push(offset + index);
    }
    cells.push(cell);
    if (cells[0]?.content === '') { cells.shift(); }
    if (cells[cells.length - 1]?.content === '') { cells.pop(); }
    return cells.map(({ content, columns }) => {
        const start = content.length - content.trimStart().length;
        return { content: content.trim(), columns: columns.slice(start, content.trimEnd().length) };
    });
}
```

Wrap the existing table rule rather than recognizing tables independently.
Capture row slices while blockquote/list prefixes are still reflected in the
block state. Line ends let us recover raw columns despite normalized newlines.
Only emitted cells get maps: extra body cells are ignored and padded cells empty.

```ts : <<symbol table mapping>>=
function tableParser(sources: WeakMap<Token, TableSource>): MarkdownIt {
    const md = new MarkdownIt();
    const table: (state: StateBlock, start: number, end: number, silent: boolean) => boolean =
        require('markdown-it/lib/rules_block/table');
    md.block.ruler.at('table', (state, start, end, silent) => {
        const count = state.tokens.length;
        const accepted = table(state, start, end, silent);
        if (!accepted || silent) { return accepted; }
        let line = start, cells: TableCell[] = [], index = 0;
        for (const token of state.tokens.slice(count)) {
            if (token.type === 'tr_open' && token.map) {
                line = token.map[0];
                const begin = state.bMarks[line] + state.tShift[line];
                const raw = state.src.slice(begin, state.eMarks[line]);
                const lineStart = state.src.lastIndexOf('\n', state.eMarks[line] - 1) + 1;
                const offset = begin - lineStart + raw.length - raw.trimStart().length;
                cells = tableCells(raw.trim(), offset);
                index = 0;
            } else if (token.type === 'inline') {
                const cell = cells[index++];
                if (cell && cell.content === token.content) { sources.set(token, { ...cell, line }); }
            }
        }
        return accepted;
    }, { alt: ['paragraph', 'reference'] });
    return md;
}
```

Parse the transformed cell for Markdown semantics, then recover each tag's raw
slice before constructing an occurrence. This keeps link destinations, images
and escaped tags excluded, and preserves literal spelling even when an escaped
pipe lies inside a name. No search for a name or cell text is involved.

```ts : <<symbol table mentions>>=
function tableOccurrences(token: Token, source: TableSource, path: string,
    lines: string[]): FragmentOccurrence[] {
    const tags: InlineTag[] = [];
    const md = mentionParser(tags, token.content);
    md.inline.parse(token.content, md, {}, []);
    return tags.flatMap(tag => {
        const column = source.columns[tag.start], endColumn = source.columns[tag.end - 1] + 1;
        const raw = lines[source.line].slice(column, endColumn);
        const original = scanFragmentTags(raw)[0];
        if (!original || original.start !== 0 || original.end !== raw.length) { return []; }
        return [occurrence(original, path, source.line, column, 'mention')];
    });
}
```

HTML blocks are conservatively excluded using a second block parse with HTML
enabled solely to identify their ranges. Normal collection still uses default
Markdown-it. Ordinary/example fences, indented code, malformed named fences and
SETTINGS bodies are never prose. Inline HTML is left to default Markdown-it
semantics; this is not an HTML attribute rename tool.

```ts : <<symbol document collection>>=
function documentOccurrences(document: SourceDocument): FragmentOccurrence[] {
    const lines = document.text.split(/\r\n|\r|\n/);
    const tables = new WeakMap<Token, TableSource>();
    const tokens = tableParser(tables).parse(document.text, {});
    const html = new MarkdownIt({ html: true }).parse(document.text, {})
        .filter(token => token.type === 'html_block' && token.map).map(token => token.map!);
    const result: FragmentOccurrence[] = [];
    for (const token of tokens) {
        if (token.type === 'fence') { result.push(...fenceOccurrences(token, document.path, lines)); }
        if (token.type === 'inline') {
            const source = tables.get(token);
            const mentions = source ? tableOccurrences(token, source, document.path, lines) :
                proseOccurrences(token, document.path, lines);
            result.push(...mentions.filter(item => !html.some(([start, end]) => item.line >= start && item.line < end)));
        }
    }
    return result.sort((a, b) => a.line - b.line || a.column - b.column);
}
```

Results follow caller document order and source order within each document.
Missing fragment references remain available for editor create commands.

```ts : <<symbol collection entry point>>=
export function collectFragmentOccurrences(documents: SourceDocument[]): FragmentOccurrence[] {
    return normalizeDocuments(documents).flatMap(documentOccurrences);
}
```

## Rename is an atomic plan

Both names must be nonblank, trimmed, single-line names without chevrons or
control characters. Quotes, dollar signs and other literal text are allowed.
A changed name must not contain backticks: these invalidate backtick fence info
strings and can change inline-code boundaries. This conservative policy also
applies to tilde-fenced definitions. Existing backtick names can be renamed away
or kept by a validated no-op; collection and the shared grammar remain literal.
The root suffix is semantic: renaming cannot add or remove `.*`, and a root
must have a nonblank name before that suffix. Rejecting a
collision with *any* known occurrence prevents capturing unresolved references
or prose mentions as well as existing definitions and append-only declarations.

```ts : <<symbol rename validation>>=
function validateRenameName(name: string, label: string): void {
    const stem = name.endsWith('.*') ? name.slice(0, -2) : name;
    if (!stem.trim() || name !== name.trim() || /[<>\x00-\x1f\x7f-\x9f\u2028\u2029]/.test(name)) {
        throw new Error(`Invalid ${label} fragment name: expected a nonempty trimmed name without chevrons or controls`);
    }
}
```

Preview edits only in fresh, normalized documents. Apply each document's edits
in descending position order so earlier raw UTF-16 coordinates remain valid.
Captured line separators preserve CRLF, CR and mixed endings exactly; neither
caller buffers nor the returned source-order edit plan are mutated.

```ts : <<symbol rename preview>>=
function previewRename(documents: SourceDocument[], edits: FragmentRenameEdit[]): SourceDocument[] {
    return documents.map(document => {
        const parts = document.text.split(/(\r\n|\r|\n)/);
        const descending = edits.filter(edit => edit.path === document.path)
            .sort((a, b) => b.line - a.line || b.startColumn - a.startColumn);
        for (const edit of descending) {
            const index = edit.line * 2, line = parts[index];
            parts[index] = line.slice(0, edit.startColumn) + edit.newText + line.slice(edit.endColumn);
        }
        return { path: document.path, text: parts.join('') };
    });
}
```

Names can change Markdown structure outside a tag, notably by introducing table
separators. Recollect the preview and require the entire ordered occurrence
sequence to survive, including unrelated symbols. Only the old name may change;
paths, lines and kinds must match. Columns may shift as names grow or shrink.
This is a syntax-preservation check, not a blanket punctuation ban.

Rename safety is presented in [Fragments as editor symbols](fragments_as_editor_symbols.html).

A no-op still validates documents, names and the unique base definition. Edits
are returned in source order for adapters to apply atomically (or in reverse
position order when manipulating strings). No replacement-string evaluation is
performed: `newText` is always the literal requested name.

The planner checks the entire recollected sequence before returning a changed
rename. Any failed validation throws without changing caller buffers; the
validated no-op returns before previewing edits.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Names[Validate names and preserve root suffix] --> Collect[Normalize documents and collect occurrences]
    Collect --> Base[Require exactly one old-name base definition]
    Base --> Same{Names equal?}
    Same -->|Yes| Noop[Return empty edit plan]
    Same -->|No| Target[Reject new-name backticks and collisions with any occurrence]
    Target --> Edits[Build name-only edits for all old-name occurrences]
    Edits --> Preview[Apply edits to copies in descending position order]
    Preview --> Recollect[Recollect all occurrences from preview]
    Recollect --> Safe{Ordered sequence preserved except renamed names?}
    Safe -->|No| Reject[Throw unsafe rename error]
    Safe -->|Yes| Return[Return source-order edits for adapter to apply]
```

```ts : <<symbol rename planning>>=
export function planFragmentRename(documents: SourceDocument[], oldName: string, newName: string): FragmentRenameEdit[] {
    validateRenameName(oldName, 'old');
    validateRenameName(newName, 'new');
    if (oldName.endsWith('.*') !== newName.endsWith('.*')) {
        throw new Error('Fragment rename must preserve the root suffix .*');
    }
    const normalized = normalizeDocuments(documents);
    const occurrences = collectFragmentOccurrences(normalized);
    const definitions = occurrences.filter(item => item.name === oldName && item.kind === 'definition');
    if (definitions.length !== 1) {
        throw new Error(`Expected exactly one base definition for ${oldName}; found ${definitions.length}`);
    }
    if (oldName === newName) { return []; }
    if (newName.includes('`')) {
        throw new Error('Invalid new fragment name: backticks are unsafe in fence headers and inline code');
    }
    if (occurrences.some(item => item.name === newName)) {
        throw new Error(`Fragment name collision: ${newName} already occurs in the project`);
    }
    const edits = occurrences.filter(item => item.name === oldName).map(item => ({
        path: item.path, line: item.line, startColumn: item.column + 2,
        endColumn: item.endColumn - 2, newText: newName
    }));
    validateRenameSyntax(normalized, edits, occurrences, oldName, newName);
    return edits;
}
```
